Rule 4(2) provides that a plaint filed under sub‐rule (1) shall also contain the following particulars:‐‐

(a) name of the Court in which the suit is brought and the fact showing that it has jurisdiction;

(b) the name, description and place of residence of the plaintiff;

(c) the name, description and place of residence of the defendant so far as can be ascertained;

(d) where the plaintiff or the defendant is a minor or a person of unsound mind a statement to that effect;

(e) the facts constituting the cause of action and the place where and date when it arose; and

(f) the nature of the claim and valuation of the claim with particulars in brief and relief claimed.

Rule 5 states that where a plaint is presented to a Court not having jurisdiction, the plaint shall be returned to be presented in the Court to which it should have been presented with an endorsement of the Court and a brief statement of the reasons therefor. The Family Court is, therefore, a Court of law as contemplated in Article 175 of the Constitution, sub‐Article (2) of which states that no Court shall have any jurisdiction save as is or max, be conferred on it by the Constitution or by or under any law.
